In order to make this explicit; you can consider returning a "tuple" from the methods instead of channel and eventloop group individually, eg:

```java

    public static final class Tuple<T extends Channel, EG extends EventLoopGroup> {
        private final Class<? extends T> channel;
        private final EG eventloopGroup;

        private Tuple(final Class<? extends T> channel, final EG eventloopGroup) {
            this.channel = channel;
            this.eventloopGroup = eventloopGroup;
        }

        public Class<? extends T> getChannel() {
            return channel;
        }

        public EG getEventloopGroup() {
            return eventloopGroup;
        }

        public void configureBootstrap(AbstractBootstrap<?, T> bootstrap) {
            bootstrap.channel(channel);
            bootstrap.group(eventloopGroup);
        }
    }

    public static Tuple<SocketChannel, EventLoopGroup> socket() {
        Class<? extends SocketChannel> c = socketChannel();
        EventLoopGroup eg = newEventLoopGroup();
        return new Tuple<>(c, eg);
    }

    public static Tuple<ServerSocketChannel, EventLoopGroup> serverSocket() {
        Class<? extends ServerSocketChannel> c = serverSocketChannel();
        EventLoopGroup eg = newEventLoopGroup();
        return new Tuple<>(c, eg);
    }
```

This also provides an opportunity to help setup the bootstrap as demonstrated above in `Tuple#configureBootstrap()`

A negative is that the number of static methods will have a combinatorial factor based on arguments to create an eventloop group but they would not be out of control 😄
